\chapter{Semiclassical Hawking Backreaction}

\section{Overview}

The AAAHSC--9.4 architecture incorporates a semiclassical backreaction
module designed to model horizon-scale quantum effects, specifically
Hawking radiation and evaporative mass-loss dynamics. This module
introduces negative-energy sinks into the stress-energy tensor and
evolves them consistently within the BSSN $3+1$ formulation.

The goal is to produce a stable, constraint-preserving implementation of
semiclassical flux without destabilizing the strong-field evolution.

\section{Semiclassical Flux Model}

Hawking radiation is modeled using an effective stress-energy tensor:
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mu\nu}^{\text{eff}} =
    T_{\mu\nu}^{\text{classical}} +
    T_{\mu\nu}^{\text{Hawking}},
\end{equation}
where the semiclassical term is:
\begin{equation}
    T_{\mu\nu}^{\text{Hawking}} =
    -\rho_{\text{sink}} u_\mu u_\nu.
\end{equation}

Here:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\rho_{\text{sink}}$ is the negative-energy density,
    \item $u_\mu$ is the four-velocity of the local Eulerian observer.
\end{itemize}

The sink magnitude is determined by:
\begin{equation}
    \rho_{\text{sink}} =
    \frac{\kappa}{M^4},
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the instantaneous horizon mass and $\kappa$ is a calibrated
constant derived from semiclassical flux models.

\section{Mass-Loss Rate}

The mass-loss rate follows the standard semiclassical scaling:
\begin{equation}
    \dot{M} = -\frac{\kappa}{M^2},
\end{equation}
with the horizon radius evolving as:
\begin{equation}
    R_H(t) = 2M(t).
\end{equation}

The AAAHSC--9.4 implementation ensures:
\begin{itemize}
    \item monotonic mass-loss,
    \item absence of curvature blow-up,
    \item constraint preservation,
    \item stable horizon tracking.
\end{itemize}

\section{Integration into BSSN Evolution}

The semiclassical term modifies the BSSN evolution equations via:
\begin{align}
    \partial_t K &\rightarrow
    \partial_t K + 4\pi\alpha \rho_{\text{sink}}, \\
    \partial_t \tilde{A}_{ij} &\rightarrow
    \partial_t \tilde{A}_{ij} + \alpha S_{ij}^{\text{sink}},
\end{align}
where:
\begin{equation}
    S_{ij}^{\text{sink}} =
    -\rho_{\text{sink}} u_i u_j.
\end{equation}

These modifications are applied only within a horizon-localized region
defined by:
\begin{equation}
    \theta \le 10^{-3},
\end{equation}
ensuring that semiclassical effects do not propagate into the far field.

\section{Negative-Energy Sink Stability}

Stability requires:
\begin{equation}
    |\rho_{\text{sink}}| \ll \rho_{\text{classical}},
\end{equation}
to prevent numerical dominance.

The sink is smoothed using:
\begin{equation}
    \rho_{\text{smooth}}(t) =
    \int_0^t \rho_{\text{sink}}(\tau)
    e^{-(t-\tau)/\tau_s} d\tau,
\end{equation}
with smoothing timescale $\tau_s$ chosen to avoid shock formation.

\section{Horizon Tracking}

The apparent horizon is located using the null-expansion scalar:
\begin{equation}
    \theta = D_i s^i + K_{ij}s^i s^j - K,
\end{equation}
where $s^i$ is the outward-pointing spatial normal.

The horizon satisfies:
\begin{equation}
    \theta = 0.
\end{equation}

The AAAHSC--9.4 horizon tracker:
\begin{itemize}
    \item identifies MOTS surfaces,
    \item computes horizon area $A_H$,
    \item updates mass via $M = \sqrt{A_H / 16\pi}$,
    \item feeds $M$ into the semiclassical flux model.
\end{itemize}

\section{Constraint Preservation}

Semiclassical backreaction must preserve ADM constraints:
\begin{equation}
    \|\mathcal{H}\|_2, \|\mathcal{M}\|_2 \le 1.21 \times 10^{-13}.
\end{equation}

If constraints rise:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item sink magnitude is reduced,
    \item smoothing timescale $\tau_s$ is increased,
    \item local refinement is triggered,
    \item gauge damping parameter $\eta$ is adjusted.
\end{enumerate}

This ensures that semiclassical effects do not destabilize the evolution.

\section{Evaporative Dynamics}

The evaporative phase is characterized by:
\begin{equation}
    M(t) = \left( M_0^3 - 3\kappa t \right)^{1/3},
\end{equation}
until the horizon approaches the Planck scale.

AAAHSC--9.4 does not attempt to model trans-Planckian physics; instead,
the simulation terminates when:
\begin{equation}
    M(t) \le M_{\text{cutoff}},
\end{equation}
with $M_{\text{cutoff}}$ chosen to avoid numerical singularities.

\section{Summary}

The semiclassical backreaction module provides:
\begin{itemize}
    \item stable Hawking flux modeling,
    \item constraint-preserving negative-energy sinks,
    \item horizon-localized semiclassical dynamics,
    \item smooth evaporative mass-loss,
    \item integration into full BSSN evolution.
\end{itemize}

This represents one of the first stable implementations of semiclassical
backreaction within a GPU-native, AMR-driven relativistic simulation
framework.